<% survey = @show.copy_cost_survey %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  The programme can say when a world crosses into self-replication and
  <%= link_to "whether it stays there", finding_path("emergence-can-be-left") %>, but nothing
  in it has yet asked whether what emerged gets any <em>better</em>. Selection, if it is
  running at all, has something to push on: a replicator that finishes its copy in fewer
  interpreter steps spends less of an interaction's budget on it, and in a fixed budget that
  is the difference between copying and running out of time. So the question is the plainest
  version of adaptation this substrate allows — once a world holds replicators, does the
  cost of a copy fall as the epochs go by?
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The engine's replicator test already runs four trials of <span class="mono">T ++ R</span>
  per tested tape and counts the ones that leave <span class="mono">T</span> in the second
  half. It now also keeps what each passing trial cost in interpreter steps and reports the
  median of them: <strong>copy cost</strong>, the vocabulary the design record's evolution
  programme fixes, measured on the runs the test was already performing rather than on new
  ones. The sample records the cost of the <em>dominant</em> replicator — the most populous
  tape among the <span class="mono">top_k</span> tested that passes the test — and null when
  no tested tape replicates. The hand-written replicator in the engine's own test suite
  copies itself in 1 794 steps, which is what pins the reading.
</p>
<p>
  This page then reads that series back out of the lab: every transitioned run, whichever
  sweep queued it, its samples at or after its own crossing epoch, in epoch order. A run is
  counted as falling when its last recorded cost is below its first post-transition one,
  rising when it is above, flat when they are equal. Counts of directions, nothing fitted.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<% if survey.any? %>
  <p>
    <strong>
      <% if survey.compared_count.zero? %>
        No run has two readings of the cost yet, so nothing can be compared.
      <% elsif survey.fell_count > survey.rose_count %>
        More transitioned worlds end up copying themselves more cheaply than end up copying
        themselves more dearly.
      <% elsif survey.fell_count.zero? %>
        No transitioned world in the lab ends cheaper than it started.
      <% else %>
        The direction is not one way: as many worlds get dearer as get cheaper.
      <% end %>
    </strong>
    The lab has <%= pluralize survey.transitioned_count, "terminal run" %> that crossed, with a
    copy cost recorded after the crossing in <%= survey.measured_count %> of them, across
    <%= pluralize survey.sweeps.size, "sweep" %>.
    <% if survey.compared_count.positive? %>
      Among the <%= pluralize survey.compared_count, "run" %> with at least two readings, the
      split is <%= survey.fell_count %> ending below their first post-transition cost,
      <%= survey.rose_count %> above it and <%= survey.flat_count %> on it.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <div id="copy-cost-runs" class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region"
       aria-label="Copy cost of every transitioned run that recorded one">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Sweep</th>
          <th>Run</th>
          <th class="numeric">Transition epoch</th>
          <th class="numeric">Readings</th>
          <th class="numeric">First cost (epoch)</th>
          <th class="numeric">Last cost (epoch)</th>
          <th class="numeric">Cheapest</th>
          <th>Direction</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% survey.table_rows.each do |row| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to row.experiment.name, experiment_path(row.experiment) %></td>
            <td><%= link_to "seed #{row.seed}", run_path(row.run) %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.transition_epoch %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.readings %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.first_cost %>
              (<%= number_with_delimiter row.first_epoch %>)
            </td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.last_cost %>
              (<%= number_with_delimiter row.last_epoch %>)
            </td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.min_cost %></td>
            <td>
              <% if row.fell? %>
                <span class="badge badge-success">cheaper</span>
              <% elsif row.rose? %>
                <span class="badge badge-warning">dearer</span>
              <% elsif row.flat? %>
                <span class="badge badge-info">unchanged</span>
              <% else %>
                <span class="text-muted">one reading</span>
              <% end %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    One row per transitioned run whose samples carry a copy cost at or after its crossing,
    grouped by sweep. Costs are interpreter steps for one byte-exact copy by the run's
    dominant replicator at that sample; "Cheapest" is the smallest the run ever recorded.
    <% if survey.capped? %>
      The table stops at <%= pluralize Findings::ShowPage::MAX_TRANSITIONS, "row" %> and
      leaves <%= pluralize survey.rows_omitted, "further run" %> out; every one of them is
      counted in the figures above.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run in this database has recorded a copy cost yet. The observable is new, and a run
    only carries it from the moment the lab's runners started measuring it, so this table
    fills in as the worlds now on the clock reach their next sample — and stays empty for
    every run that finished before the engine could count the steps.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What this does not claim</h2>
<p>
  <strong>This is not the adaptation sweep.</strong> The design record's evolution programme
  asks for a grid designed to measure adaptation: parameter points that transition, long
  budgets, enough seeds for the direction to mean something. What is above is every run that
  happened to transition, from sweeps built to answer other questions at other budgets, none
  of them run to answer this one. A count of directions over such a set describes the set.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>"Dominant replicator" is a tape, not a lineage.</strong> The cost is read off the
  most populous tape that passes the replicator test at that sample. When it falls between
  two samples, that can be one colony getting cheaper — or a cheaper replicator taking over
  from a dearer one, which is selection too but a different sentence, and this page cannot
  tell the two apart. It also says nothing about the tapes below the top of the census.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Every series is censored at both ends.</strong> No run recorded a copy cost before
  this observable existed, so each series starts wherever the lab first ran the new engine
  rather than at the world's crossing, and ends wherever that run's budget ended. A cost
  that was falling when the clock ran out was falling, not converging.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Steps are not fitness.</strong> Copy cost is the price of one copy in a test the
  engine performs against a random partner, not the rate at which a tape actually spreads
  through the world. A cheaper copier that meets fewer partners spreads more slowly than a
  dearer one that meets more; <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> and the census remain the
  observables of what happened in situ.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The series has to accumulate before it can be read: the runs on the clock need to sample
  under the new engine for long enough to give more than a couple of readings each. After
  that, the honest form of this question is the adaptation sweep — the transitioning
  parameter points at many seeds and a long budget — and the version of it that would really
  settle the matter reads copy cost per lineage rather than per dominant tape, so a single
  colony can be watched getting cheaper.
</p>
